---
// COOKBOOK — authoring conditions and formations (docs-refactor Phase 5, #1000; plan §8).
//
// The gap list asks for condition & formation AUTHORING, not enumeration (/docs/api/declarative#conditions and
// /docs/api/declarative already enumerate them). Two honest findings shape this page:
//
//  1. There is no public registration API for a CUSTOM condition. `createRegistry()` exposes
//     `.condition(id, fn)`, but `createField` builds its own registry internally and takes no
//     registry option — so authoring means composing the six built-ins, and this page says so
//     rather than implying an extensibility that does not exist.
//  2. `data-formation` is read by the engine (field.ts's conductor scans `[data-formation]`) but
//     appears in NO documentation table, and the check:docs body-attr surface cannot see it because
//     that extractor reads scanner.ts. Documented here; noted in the PR.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SeeAlso from '../../../components/SeeAlso.astro';
import StatusChip from '../../../components/StatusChip.astro';
import CodeTabs from '../../../components/CodeTabs.astro';
import PlatformSupport from '../../../components/PlatformSupport.astro';
import CookbookExample from '../../../components/CookbookExample.astro';
import CookbookDemo from '../../../components/CookbookDemo.astro';
import { CONDITIONS, FORMATIONS } from '@fundamental-engine/core';

import formationsSrc from '../../../lib/cookbook/formations.ts?raw';
import { runFormations } from '../../../lib/cookbook/formations.ts';

const formations = runFormations();

const whenHtml = `<!-- A gate decides WHEN a body's forces act. The body is always a body;
     the gate only decides whether its force applies this frame. -->
<button data-body="repel" data-when="active" data-hot data-strength="1.4">
  Delete
</button>

<!-- Selective gates read each PARTICLE, not the body: 'fast' acts only on
     fast-moving matter, so the same body treats calm and agitated matter differently. -->
<div data-body="viscosity" data-when="fast" data-strength="0.8">Settling zone</div>`;

const formationHtml = `<!-- The conductor: as a section crosses mid-viewport it eases the
     WHOLE field into that posture. After ~6s of no input the field drifts back to 'ambient'. -->
<section data-formation="wells">   … work …   </section>
<section data-formation="lanes">   … writing … </section>`;

const formationJs = `field.setFormation('lanes');   // …or drive it directly`;
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Fundamental — Authoring conditions & formations"
  description="The two orthogonal axes of authoring: conditional gates (data-when) that decide when a body's forces act, and formations — the one global posture every free particle carries — set by the conductor via data-formation or directly with setFormation."
>
  <header class="docs-hero">
    <p class="kicker">Cookbook</p>
    <h1>Conditions &amp; <span class="accent">formations</span></h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Two axes sit beside the forces, and both are about <em>when</em> rather than <em>what</em>. A
      <strong>condition</strong> gates one body: its forces act only when the gate passes. A
      <strong>formation</strong> is field-wide: one global posture every free particle carries.
      Neither is a force, and neither can be expressed as one.
    </p>
    <StatusChip label="Shipped · closed vocabularies, stated honestly" tone="stable" />
  </header>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="conditions">Conditions — gating one body</h2>
    <p>
      <code>data-when</code> takes one of {CONDITIONS.length - 1} gate names (plus the empty default,
      "always"). The distinction that matters when authoring: <code>active</code> and
      <code>scrolling</code> read the <em>world</em> — is this body engaged, is the page moving —
      while <code>fast</code>, <code>slow</code>, <code>hot</code> and <code>cool</code> are
      <strong>selective</strong>: they are evaluated per particle, so one body can act on agitated
      matter and ignore calm matter in the same frame.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs variants={[{ label: 'HTML', lang: 'html', code: whenHtml }]} />

    <dl class="api-table">
      {
        CONDITIONS.filter((c) => c.id).map((c) => (
          <div class="api-row" id={`when-${c.id}`}>
            <dt class="k">
              <code>data-when="{c.id}"</code>
              <span class="ty">{c.selective ? 'per particle' : 'per body / frame'}</span>
            </dt>
            <dd class="d">
              {c.desc}
              <PlatformSupport dimension="conditions" symbol={c.id} />
            </dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <CookbookDemo
      kind="conditions"
      title="A gate that only opens when engaged"
      note="Hover or focus the gated body: data-when='active' means its force applies only while it is engaged. The readout reports the gate state and the live --d."
    >
      <div class="cb-chip" data-cb-gated data-body="attract" data-when="active" data-strength="1.6" data-range="220" data-feedback data-hot tabindex="0">
        gated body — hover or focus me
      </div>
      <div class="cb-chip" data-body="attract" data-strength="0.5" data-range="160" data-feedback>
        ungated neighbour
      </div>
    </CookbookDemo>

    <div class="callout">
      <b>The vocabulary is closed, and that is worth saying plainly.</b> The engine's registry does
      carry a <code>condition(id, fn)</code> method, but <code>createField</code> constructs its
      registry internally and accepts no registry option — so there is <strong>no public API for
      registering a custom gate</strong> today. Authoring a condition means composing the built-ins
      with the force set and the <code>data-affects</code> / <code>data-species</code> selectors.
      Reach for a <a href="/docs/cookbook/reading-the-field#channels">field channel</a> when the
      predicate you want is really external data.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="formations">Formations — the one global posture</h2>
    <p>
      A formation is five numbers — drift, wander, orbit, spread, convergence — applied as a bias to
      every free particle. The engine <strong>eases</strong> toward the target each frame, so a
      change glides rather than snaps. There are {FORMATIONS.length}, and the presets themselves are
      engine-owned: authoring means choosing one, and deciding <em>when</em> it applies.
    </p>
    <CodeTabs
      variants={[
        { label: 'Declarative (the conductor)', lang: 'html', code: formationHtml },
        { label: 'Imperative', lang: 'ts', code: formationJs },
      ]}
    />
    <p>
      <PlatformSupport dimension="field-handle-methods" symbol="setFormation" />
    </p>

    <CookbookExample
      title="Switching the posture is visible in the matter"
      path="apps/site/src/lib/cookbook/formations.ts"
      source={formationsSrc}
      result={formations}
      caption="Mean horizontal velocity across the live pool, measured under each posture: `ambient` rests, `lanes` carries. The formation is not a flag — it moves the matter."
    />

    <div class="callout">
      <b>A documentation gap this page closes.</b> <code>data-formation</code> is read by the engine —
      the conductor scans <code>[data-formation]</code> sections and eases the field as each crosses
      mid-viewport — but it appears in no attribute table on this site. It escaped the
      <code>check:docs</code> body-attribute surface because that gate reads the scanner, and this
      attribute is handled in the frame loop instead. Filed with the Phase 5 PR.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="docs-sec">
    <h2 id="choosing">Which axis do I want?</h2>
    <ul class="model">
      <li><b>One element should behave differently in some state</b> → a condition.</li>
      <li><b>The whole page should change character in a section</b> → a formation.</li>
      <li><b>Only some matter should respond</b> → a selective gate (<code>fast</code>, <code>hot</code>, …).</li>
      <li><b>The rule depends on your data</b> → neither: a <a href="/docs/cookbook/reading-the-field#channels">channel</a>, or a metric through <a href="/docs/cookbook/data-driven">bindData</a>.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <SeeAlso
    links={[
      { href: '/docs/cookbook', label: 'All cookbook patterns' },
      { href: '/docs/api/declarative#conditions', label: 'Conditions & formations in the reference' },
      { href: '/docs/api/declarative#conditions', label: 'The catalog page' },
      { href: '/docs/cookbook/reading-the-field', label: 'Reading & instrumenting' },
    ]}
  />

  <script>
    import { initCookbookDemos } from '../../../lib/cookbook/demos.ts';
    initCookbookDemos();
  </script>
</DocsLayout>
